    sections: [
      {
        type: 'about',
        tabLabel: 'About',
        pages: [
          {
            title: 'Texas 42',
            subtitle: 'Card Game',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Texas 42 is a four-player partnership trick-taking game using a double-six domino set (28 tiles). Each draws 7; bid 30â€“42 (points the team will take). High bidder names trump (blanks, 1sâ€“6s, doubles, or no-trump) and leads. Follow suit; else any tile. 42 points per hand: 1 per trick plus count tiles (5-5 and 6-4 = 10 each; 5-0, 4-1, 3-2 = 5 each). Meet bid to score; else opponents get bid plus points. First to 250 (or 7 marks) wins.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Country/Region of Origin: USA (Texas)\nTime Period: 1887\nHistorical Development: Invented in Trapp Spring (now Garner), Parker County, Texas, by William Thomas (12) and Walter Earl (14) as a domino alternative to card games; spread across Texas via families and communities; soldiers took it overseas in WWII.\nCultural Significance: Designated State Domino Game of Texas (2011); played in homes, churches, clubs, and tournaments.',
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Setup',
            subtitle: 'Table, deck, equipment, and deal',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: '4 players in fixed partnerships; players sit opposite their partner.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Double-six set of dominoes (28 tiles, one for each pair from 0 to 6).',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Double-six domino set; score sheet or marks (e.g. drawn to form ALL).',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Dealer shuffles face down; each player takes 7 dominoes (opponents first, then partner, then dealer). Deal rotates clockwise.',
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Variations',
            subtitle: 'Known ways this game changes',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'list',
                style: 'unordered',
                items: [
                  {
                    text: 'No Trump (Follow Me): High bidder may play without trumps; doubles high or doubles low.',
                  },
                  {
                    text: 'Doubles trump: Bidder can choose doubles as trumps; doubles form a separate suit ranking 6-6 to 0-0.',
                  },
                  {
                    text: "Nello: Contract to lose every trick; declarer's partner sits out; no trumps; doubles form own suit.",
                  },
                  {
                    text: 'Plunge: Declarer must have 4+ doubles; partner names trump and declarer leads; must take all 7 tricks. Bid 4+ marks.',
                  },
                  {
                    text: "Sevens: Each player plays tile with pip total closest to 7; declarer's team must take all 7 tricks.",
                  },
                ],
              },
            ],
          },
        ],
      },
    ],
